[ORGANIZATION] is the first paper we have looked at which is based on=20
clustering.  [ORGANIZATION] tries to established routes based on bandwidth.  =
This is=20
the performance measurement it uses and in this way it is Quality of=20
Service algorithm which tries to satisfy minimum bandwidth requirement of a=20
node.  [ORGANIZATION] has three main parts to it: finding the core graph,=20
propagation of bandwidth information amoung the core, and route=20
computation.  One of the goals with having core nodes is to minimise the=20
changes in the network core when topology changes.  Changes are only effect=20
local neighbour hoods, and other core nodes do not have to worry about this.

[ORGANIZATION] also has this idea of increase and decrease waves to denote hi=
gh=20
bandwidth stable links and short unstable low bandwidth links.  This is the=20
basis of how [ORGANIZATION] calculates routes based on bandwidth.  The idea i=
s that=20
short decrease waves are only travel far enough to be local within a=20
neighbour hood, and long increase waves are used for links between=20
neighbourhoods.  In this way, short unreliable links do not need to be=20
propagated to the whole network, where as more important stable bandwidth=20
information is.  There are many ideas in this paper that I like about=20
[ORGANIZATION], and it would be interesting to see how this algorithm work in=
 real=20
life.  Though they have many tables and results from simulations, they only=20
briefly mention their implementation on [PRODUCT].  They only briefly mention=
ed=20
that there were implementation difficulties.  What they found on the [PRODUCT=
]=20
implementation would have been interesting.

Some things research would be to see how having core nodes and effectively=20
a hierarchy effect the other properties of an ad-hoc network.  Intuitively,=20
core nodes are used more and require more computation and communication=20
power.  This probably means that they can be a bottle neck and their=20
battery power (which we have established is important) can run out quickly.=20
Perhaps the electing of a core node should take into consideration whether=20
the core can handle the work that is required from it.  And for practical=20
reasons, one would like to research, does typical ad-hoc network really=20
need this sort of structure?  Do ad-hoc networks typically get large enough=20
and stay alive long enough to require a hierarchy structure?
